# The in-mesh WARNING debt the samples trees carry, for `mw-plugin-test compile
# --warning-baseline`. Format: `<NodeType path> <diagnostic id>`, `#` comments, blanks ignored.
#
# ── THIS FILE IS DELIBERATELY EMPTY OF ENTRIES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# An EMPTY baseline is the strictest setting there is, and it is spelled as an empty FILE on
# purpose: nothing is tolerated, so EVERY in-mesh warning these trees produce fails the bake. It is
# not the same as omitting `--warning-baseline`, which would be OBSERVE-ONLY — "no known debt" and
# "the bake never read a baseline" must never render the same, which is why the file stays here
# rather than being deleted. `bake-then-gate.sh` asserts both ratchets print ENFORCED.
#
# 🚨 SHRINK-ONLY, and the gate holds you to it in BOTH directions:
#   * a (type, code) pair that is NOT listed FAILS the bake — the debt cannot grow back;
#   * a listed pair whose type compiled CLEAN of that code is STALE and FAILS the bake until the
#     line is deleted — so paying a debt down is not optional bookkeeping.
# A listed type that did NOT compile this run is UNVERIFIABLE, never stale: it produced no warnings
# at all, and reading that silence as "the debt is paid" would delete a real record on a
# measurement nobody took. With no lines at all, the first rule is the whole contract.
#
# 🚨 TWO RATCHETS, one file, split by the code — see Doc/Architecture/InMeshWarningStandard.
#   * CS1591 is documentation debt and has its own verdict, so a missing doc comment can never be
#     the reason an unrelated change cannot merge.
#   * everything else is a latent bug or a doc comment that is actively WRONG.
#
# ── WHERE THE THIRTY LINES WENT (2026-09-18) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 🚨 They were NOT fixed by writing anything. Say so plainly, because the opposite claim is the
# mistake this file exists to make impossible: every one of the 30 named CS1591 or CS1701 — the two
# families #4591 added to `CompileWarning.NotReported`, the in-mesh compile's PARITY list.
#
# The premise of the whole standard is that in-mesh C# is held to the standard `src/` C# is held to
# under `-warnaserror`. A raw CSharpCompilation applies no NoWarn at all, so without that list the
# in-mesh compile was held to a STRICTER standard than any `dotnet build` in the fleet, in exactly
# two families:
#   * CS1701/CS1702 — reference-set skew ("assuming assembly reference 'System.Linq.Expressions,
#     Version=8.0.0.0' used by 'System.Reactive' matches identity '…Version=10.0.0.0'"). A property
#     of the REFERENCE SET, never of the content: no author can fix it and no `#pragma` belongs in
#     their source. Measured on this repo at e8fce8130d: a 28-project `-c Release -warnaserror`
#     build reports `0 Warning(s)` and ZERO CS1701, with Rx referenced throughout.
#   * CS1591/CS1573/CS1712 — doc COMPLETENESS, suppressed for `src/` by core's own
#     Directory.Build.props: `<NoWarn>649;CA2255;NU5104;NU1510;CS1591;CS1573;CS1712;$(NoWarn)</NoWarn>`.
#
# The tester names such a line INERT: it tolerates nothing (nothing can produce the code) and hides
# nothing, and it prints "Delete the lines" on every run. Deleting them is that instruction carried
# out, and it leaves the file exactly consistent with what the bake measures.
#
# Measured on these trees at e8fce8130d, with `MW_LOG_LEVEL=Information`:
#
#   before  warnings: 0 raw occurrence(s) folded to 0 distinct site(s), over 0 of 27 compiled type(s)
#           warnings: 30 INERT baseline entr(ies) naming CS1591, CS1701 — … Delete the lines.
#           warnings: warnings     ratchet — ENFORCED against 0 baseline entr(ies): 0 NEW, 0 stale
#           warnings: doc-comments ratchet — ENFORCED against 0 baseline entr(ies): 0 NEW, 0 stale
#   after   the same three measured lines, and no INERT line.
#
# Both ratchets were already enforcing against ZERO entries before the trim — `WarningBaseline.For`
# excludes a retired code — so nothing this file used to say was being relied on by anything. The
# trees themselves produce NO warnings at all: 27 of 28 NodeTypes compile (PensionFund/BalanceSheet
# is the samples-gate.allow known-debt `#r "nuget:…"` that cannot resolve on a gate host) and every
# one of the 27 compiles clean.
#
# 🚨 What this file is NOT. It is not a record that the sample trees' public members are
# documented — CS1591 is suppressed, so nobody is measuring that. The 362 occurrences over 271
# members the earlier revision counted are still undocumented; writing those doc comments is worth
# doing and changes NO verdict here. Do not read an empty file as a claim that it was done.
#
# 🚨 THIS IS STILL NOT A PLACE TO PUT A DIAGNOSTIC YOU DID NOT WANT TO FIX, and three whole codes
# that this file would otherwise have carried were fixed at the ROOT instead, because the platform —
# not the content — emitted them, and baselining them would have recorded the generator's debt under
# the content's name in every repo of the fleet:
#   * CS0105 (119 occurrences / 23 types) — the skeleton generator concatenated every source
#     file's `using` lines without de-duplicating them, so correct authored source earned "the
#     using directive … appeared previously". Fixed in DynamicMeshNodeAttributeGenerator.
#   * CS1591 × 2 per type (54 sites) — the generated `…MeshNodeProviderAttribute` and its `Nodes`
#     property are public and were undocumented. No author could ever have fixed those. Fixed by
#     generating the doc comments.
#   * CS8632 (21 types) — authored `string?` in a tree with no `#nullable` directive. Fixed with
#     NullableContextOptions.Annotations (the annotation context only — no nullable analysis).
# Measured across those root fixes and this trim: 850 raw occurrences over 27 of 27 types → 375
# over 18 → 0 over 0.
#
# 🚨 ADD A LINE ONLY FOR A WARNING A RUN ACTUALLY REPORTED, and prefer fixing it.
